Yes. The strongest version of your idea is not really about sex—it’s about resolution.

A coarse model gives you coarse interventions. If the whole phenomenon is represented as one variable—“sex drive”—then your tools are basically more, less, on, off. That is the sword. Once you decompose it into distinct biological functions, you can potentially manipulate them independently. That is the scalpel.

Sexual function already appears to be decomposable into partially separable dimensions: baseline libido, spontaneous desire, responsive desire, genital arousal, subjective arousal, reward/salience, attraction, inhibition, orgasm threshold, orgasm intensity, refractory period, pair-bonding, novelty seeking, anxiety, satiation, and so forth. Those aren’t controlled by one “sex hormone”; they emerge from interacting endocrine, autonomic, sensory, reward, and cognitive systems.

And we already have crude proof that these dimensions can move independently. Testosterone can alter libido without guaranteeing erections. PDE5 inhibitors can improve erection physiology without creating desire. SSRIs can leave attraction intact while suppressing orgasm or libido. Dopaminergic drugs can sometimes increase sexual motivation or compulsive sexual behavior. Prolactin participates in post-orgasmic satiety. Melanocortin signaling affects sexual arousal. So nature is already telling us, these are different knobs.

Your GLP-1 analogy therefore works at the architectural level. Hunger used to look like a simple subjective state—“I am hungry.” Then researchers progressively distinguished gastric signaling, glucose regulation, insulin, leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, hypothalamic circuits, reward eating, satiation, and satiety. Once the state was decomposed mechanistically, pharmacology gained much finer handles.

Sexuality could undergo a similar progression:

“horny/not horny”
→ component taxonomy
→ measurable biomarkers/circuits
→ causal model
→ selective targets
→ tunable interventions

There is one important distinction, though. Vocabulary doesn’t itself create the biological control. It creates the representation that makes systematic discovery possible. You still need measurements, causal experiments, receptor/pathway identification, dose-response models, and safety data. Naming a distinction is like adding an address to the map; now science can repeatedly travel to the same place.

And “what you’re into” sits farther downstream than something like appetite intensity. Preferences and attractions involve development, memory, reinforcement, context, identity, learning, hormones, and neural reward systems. We currently have neither a clean mechanistic decomposition nor a safe, reliable way to dial specific attractions up and down. It would also create substantial ethical issues if such technology were ever developed.

But your larger principle survives that caveat:

Intervention precision cannot exceed representation precision.

Or in your tool metaphor:

Undifferentiated phenomenon → sword.
Taxonomy → tool chest.
Causal anatomy → scalpel.
Independent control variables → surgery.

And that principle generalizes almost perfectly to cognition. “Attention,” “confidence,” “fear,” “motivation,” or even “intelligence” are probably sword-level words too. Break each into its actual operators and feedback loops, and suddenly what looked like an indivisible personality trait becomes an engineering surface.